---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const B = (import.meta.env.BASE_URL || '/').replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Owl Cam — know what happened in the room while you were out" description="Turn a Mac and any webcam or iPhone into a local security camera. Records only when something happens, keeps recording while someone is there, and nothing ever leaves the Mac. Free for one camera, $29 once for the rest." lang="en" path="/">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-glow"></div>
    <div class="wrap hero-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">macOS · menu bar · nothing leaves your Mac</span>
      <h1>Know what happened in the room while you were out.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Owl Cam turns a Mac and any webcam or iPhone into a camera that records only when something happens — and keeps recording as long as someone is in the room. The video stays on the Mac.</p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn primary big" href={`${B}/download`}>Download for Mac</a>
        <a class="btn big" href="#pricing">See pricing</a>
      </div>
      <div class="trust">
        <span>✓ <b>Notarized by Apple</b></span>
        <span>✓ <b>No account, no cloud</b></span>
        <span>✓ <b>Free for one camera</b></span>
        <span>macOS 14+ · Apple Silicon &amp; Intel</span>
      </div>
      <div class="shot"><img src={`${B}/shot-live.jpg`} alt="Owl Cam live view with four cameras: two recording with a person in frame, one finishing a clip, one idle" width="1440" height="901" loading="eager" /></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section id="who">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who it's for</span>
      <h2>You already own the camera. It's the Mac you're not using.</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card"><span class="k">The desk you leave behind</span><h3>Out for the afternoon</h3><p>You want to know whether anyone came near your desk while you were gone — and see it, rather than wonder.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">The Mac on the shelf</span><h3>An old MacBook, still working</h3><p>Point it at the front door, the cot, the workshop. It costs nothing to try and it was going to sit in a drawer anyway.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">The counter</span><h3>A shop, studio or clinic</h3><p>An iMac already faces the room. An iPhone on a stand adds the second angle, without a subscription or a new box on the wall.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="limits" style="margin-top:18px">
        <div><b>Not you?</b> If you want to open an app at the office and watch a live feed, or get a push the second someone walks in, Owl Cam is the wrong tool. It records for you to review later — it does not stream and does not notify.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="how" style="padding-top:12px">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">The problem</span>
      <h2>Motion recorders cut you off.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Sit still for ten seconds and the clip ends. Move again and a new one starts. An evening at the desk becomes forty fragments, and the moment you actually want is split across two of them.</p>
      <div class="compare">
        <div class="them"><span class="k">Typical motion recording</span><div class="n">14 clips</div><p>One person, one hour at a desk. Three of the clips are under five seconds. Finding the moment means opening all of them.</p></div>
        <div class="us"><span class="k">Owl Cam</span><div class="n">1 clip</div><p>The same hour, one file, starting three seconds before the first movement and ending when the room is genuinely empty again.</p></div>
      </div>
      <h2 style="margin-top:28px">Three readings, three lines.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Owl Cam keeps a frozen picture of the empty room and compares every frame against it — so a person who stops moving is still obviously there.</p>
      <div class="rule"><span class="dot" style="background:#ff453a"></span><p><b>Movement → record.</b> Frame-to-frame change. Fast, immune to a light being switched on. It can only start a clip, never end one.</p></div>
      <div class="rule"><span class="dot" style="background:#ff9f0a"></span><p><b>Something present → record.</b> Difference from the empty scene. Stays high while anything new is in the room — a person, an animal, a parcel — even if it holds still.</p></div>
      <div class="rule"><span class="dot" style="background:#30d158"></span><p><b>Below this → stop.</b> The clip ends only after the reading falls under the line and stays there. And never while Apple's on-device Vision still recognises a person.</p></div>
      <div class="shot"><img src={`${B}/shot-detection.png`} alt="Detection settings with live charts for the three thresholds" width="1440" height="1408" loading="lazy" /></div>
      <p class="btn-note">Every number above is yours to move, and the charts update as you drag. There is nothing hidden behind an "AI sensitivity" slider.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span>
      <h2>The video never leaves your Mac.</h2>
      <p class="lede">This is the whole reason the app exists on your machine instead of in someone's cloud. It is also the one claim you should not take on faith — so here is how to check it yourself.</p>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Storage</span><h3>Files, in a folder you chose</h3><p>Plain <code>.mp4</code> files you can open in QuickTime, move, or delete. No database, no proprietary container, no vendor lock-in.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Detection</span><h3>On this Mac's chip</h3><p>Motion analysis and Apple's Vision person detection run locally, on frames that are never written anywhere but your recordings.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Network</span><h3>One request, only if you buy</h3><p>The single outbound call is validating a Pro licence key. No account, no sign-up, no analytics, no crash reporting. The free version never calls out at all.</p></div>
      </div>
      <pre class="verify"><b># Confirm the app is signed and notarized by Apple</b>
codesign -dvv /Applications/Owl\ Cam.app
spctl -a -vv -t install /Applications/Owl\ Cam.app

<b># Then watch it: with a network monitor such as LuLu or Little Snitch,</b>
<b># the free version makes no outbound connection at all.</b></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="features" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">What's in the box</span>
      <h2>Small app. Every piece earns its place.</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Pre-roll</span><h3>Starts before it started</h3><p>A rolling buffer puts the first seconds of every event into the file, instead of losing them while the detector made up its mind.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Review</span><h3>Calendar → timeline → frames</h3><p>Days with people in them are marked. Drag a span on the timeline to get thumbnails; click one to play from that second.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Cameras</span><h3>USB, built-in, iPhone</h3><p>Any camera macOS can see, Continuity Camera included. Each one detects and records independently, into its own folder.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Timestamp</span><h3>Burned in, exact</h3><p>Each frame carries the moment it was actually captured — pre-roll included — in the position, size and format you choose.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Storage</span><h3>A budget, with protection rules</h3><p>Cap by size or by free space. Delete oldest, smallest, or clips without people first — and keep anything recent, large or marked.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Backup</span><h3>NAS, with a USB fallback</h3><p>Copies finished clips off the Mac and verifies them byte for byte. If the NAS is asleep, the USB drive takes over and the NAS catches up later.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Backup rules</span><h3>Only what's worth keeping</h3><p>Copy a clip only if it matches: two people at once, longer than five minutes, over 60 MB, at night, from that one camera.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="k">Schedule</span><h3>Quiet hours</h3><p>Pause by weekday and time window. A recording in progress is never cut short; it simply won't start a new one.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">Honest limits</span>
      <h2>What it doesn't do.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Better to read this now than to find out after paying.</p>
      <div class="limits">
        <div><b>No alerts</b> There is no phone notification when someone walks in. You review afterwards.</div>
        <div><b>No streaming</b> You watch the live picture and the recordings on that Mac. There is no remote viewing app.</div>
        <div><b>No IP cameras</b> RTSP and ONVIF are not supported — only cameras macOS itself can see.</div>
        <div><b>iPhone has strings</b> Continuity Camera needs the iPhone locked, charging and near the Mac. A MacBook's built-in camera stops when you close the lid.</div>
        <div><b>Cards only</b> Payment is by Visa or Mastercard through Polar. Alipay and WeChat Pay are not available yet.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="pricing" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">Pricing</span>
      <h2>Free for one camera. $29 once for the rest.</h2>
      <p class="lede">A local app has no servers to pay for every month, so it doesn't ask you to. Buy it once, keep it.</p>
      <div class="pricing">
        <div class="plan">
          <h3>Free</h3>
          <div class="price">$0 <small>forever</small></div>
          <ul>
            <li>One camera</li><li>Movement + presence detection, pre-roll</li><li>On-device person detection</li><li>Calendar &amp; timeline review</li><li>Built-in detection presets</li>
            <li class="no">Multiple cameras</li><li class="no">Timestamp, backup, storage budget</li><li class="no">Scheduled pauses, custom presets</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="btn" href={`${B}/download`}>Download</a>
        </div>
        <div class="plan pro">
          <h3>Pro</h3>
          <div class="price">$29 <small>one-time · 2 Macs</small></div>
          <ul>
            <li>Everything in Free</li><li>Unlimited cameras</li><li>Burned-in timestamp</li><li>Auto backup to NAS / USB, with rules</li><li>Storage budget with protection rules</li><li>Per-camera settings</li><li>Scheduled pauses (quiet hours)</li><li>Save your own detection presets</li><li>Email support</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="btn primary" href={`${B}/buy`}>Buy Pro</a>
          <p class="btn-note">14-day full trial on first launch, no account. 14-day refund, no questions. VAT handled by Polar.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap sec-in">
      <span class="eyebrow">FAQ</span>
      <h2>Fair questions</h2>
      <details open><summary>Does any video leave my Mac?</summary><p>No. Detection, recording and review are local, and recordings are plain files in a folder you picked. The only outbound request in the whole app is validating a Pro key — the free version makes none. Backups go wherever you point them, including a drive with no internet at all.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Will it stop recording while I'm sitting still?</summary><p>That is the one thing it is built not to do. The scene reading stays high while you are in the room, and Vision vetoes every stop while it recognises a person. The only exit is a hard cap you set yourself: fully still for N seconds with nobody recognised.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is it signed by Apple?</summary><p>Yes. Every build is signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple, so macOS opens it normally — no right-click workaround, and no repeated camera-permission prompts after an update.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Which cameras work?</summary><p>Anything macOS exposes as a camera: USB webcams (developed against the Insta360 Link 2C), the built-in camera, and an iPhone over Continuity Camera. Each camera runs its own detection and writes to its own folder.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How much disk does it use?</summary><p>You choose the quality; the settings show the resulting bitrate and an estimate per hour. Then set a storage budget and Owl Cam holds itself to it, deleting by rules you pick rather than filling the disk.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What if I don't like it?</summary><p>The 14-day trial is the whole app, no account and no card. If you buy and it's not right, email within 14 days and Polar refunds it.</p></details>
      <p class="btn-note" style="margin-top:14px"><a href={`${B}/faq`}>More questions →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="get" style="padding-top:0;padding-bottom:96px">
    <div class="wrap sec-in" style="align-items:center;text-align:center">
      <h2>Point a Mac at the room and find out.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin:0 auto">One camera is free for as long as you want it. Nothing to sign up for.</p>
      <div class="cta-row" style="margin-top:6px">
        <a class="btn primary big" href={`${B}/download`}>Download for Mac</a>
      </div>
      <p class="btn-note">Notarized by Apple · macOS 14 or later · Universal (Apple Silicon &amp; Intel) · <a href="https://github.com/wuyumo/owlcam-releases/releases">release notes</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
